# JVM Report

## Introduction
Java runs on the Java Virtual Machine (JVM), which executes bytecode and makes Java platform-independent.

## 1. Class Loader
Loads `.class` files into memory.

**Types:**
- Bootstrap ClassLoader loads core Java classes
- Extension ClassLoader loads extension libraries
- Application ClassLoader loads application classes

**Flow:**
.java → compiled → .class → loaded into JVM

## 2. Runtime Data Areas

**Heap**
- Stores objects
- Shared across threads

**Stack**
- Stores method calls & local variables
- Each thread has its own stack

**Method Area**
- Stores class metadata & static variables

**PC Register**
- Stores current executing instruction

## 3. Execution Engine

Executes bytecode using:
- **Interpreter** – executes line by line (slow)
- **JIT Compiler** – converts to machine code (fast)

## 4. JIT vs Interpreter

Interpreter - JIT Compiler
Slower - Faster
Line-by-line - Compiled
No optimization - Optimized

## 5. Write Once, Run Anywhere
Java code compiles to **bytecode**, which runs on any JVM.
**Flow:**
Write code → Compile → Bytecode → Run on any system

## Conclusion
JVM handles class loading, memory management, and execution, enabling Java’s platform independence and performance.